# Each phrase leaves the reader to supply a criterion the writer knows
# (`set the timeout as needed`), so the fix names it (`set the timeout
# longer than the slowest request`) or drops the phrase when every
# case applies. The Optional rule of the same name holds the adjectives
# and adverbs (`appropriate`, `properly`).
#
# `as required` counts only without `by` after it, because `as required
# by the spec` names the criterion. The guard sits glued to `required`
# so it reads the token right after it, which also lets the phrase at
# the end of a line count.
#
# It hit 35 lines in the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, and 14 of 15
# sampled hits left the criterion unstated. The miss described lazy
# evaluation (`computes values when needed`), where the phrase names
# the criterion.
expr main [(as needed), (as necessary), (as appropriate), (as applicable), (as required!(( )by)), (where needed), (where necessary), (where appropriate), (where applicable), (when needed), (when necessary), (when appropriate), (when applicable), (if needed), (if necessary), (if appropriate), (if applicable)]
let message "Vague criterion. Name the condition the reader should check, or drop the phrase."
let description "Flags as needed, where applicable, if necessary, and similar phrases that leave the reader to supply a criterion."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Set the timeout longer than the slowest request."
allows "Pin the version as required by the release policy."
